## 1. Result and Boundary

Let \(X\) be the selected q79 Fu-Yau principal elliptic threefold and let

\[
0\longrightarrow\mathcal O_X(-H)\longrightarrow
\kappa_{\rm hol}\longrightarrow E\longrightarrow0 \tag{1}
\]

be the rank \(1+2\) filtration dual to the explicit Hartshorne-Serre
construction. Then

\[
\dim\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(E,\mathcal O_X(-H))=1, \tag{2}
\]

and

\[
\dim\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(\mathcal O_X(-H),E)\ge1. \tag{3}
\]

Equation (2) proves that the non-split forward Hartshorne-Serre extension is
unique up to scale. Equation (3) proves that at least one non-split reverse
extension exists:

\[
0\longrightarrow E\longrightarrow\kappa_{\rm rev}\longrightarrow
\mathcal O_X(-H)\longrightarrow0. \tag{4}
\]

Every such reverse middle object has the same K-class and Chern character as
\(\kappa_{\rm hol}\). This promotes two candidate statements in the latest
q79 handoff to repository-backed exact results.

It does not select a reverse class, prove that its middle term is locally
free, establish full \(E[3]\) or shared-line equivariance, compute its fiber
restrictions, or show that its corrected transform is the physical hidden
\(W_9\). Those are the remaining constructive tests.

## 2. The Dual Hartshorne-Serre Sequence

Write

\[
W=\omega_\Gamma\otimes\mathcal O_X(-H)|_\Gamma.
\]

The exact dual of the selected Hartshorne-Serre extension contains

\[
0\longrightarrow E\longrightarrow\mathcal O_X^{\oplus2}
\longrightarrow i_*W\longrightarrow0. \tag{5}
\]

Here \(\Gamma\) is the connected codimension-two lci curve already
constructed in the q79 source packet. It has arithmetic genus seven. The
same exact sequence produces (1); in particular

\[
\operatorname{ch}(E)=2-3u,
\qquad
[\kappa_{\rm hol}]=[\mathcal O_X(-H)]+[E]. \tag{6}
\]

The curve is Gorenstein because it is lci, so \(W\) is invertible.

## 3. Forward Ext Rigidity

Put \(L_0=\mathcal O_X(-H)\). Since \(i:\Gamma\hookrightarrow X\) is a
codimension-two regular embedding and \(K_X\simeq\mathcal O_X\), local
duality gives

\[
R\mathcal Hom_X(i_*W,L_0)
\simeq i_*\mathcal O_\Gamma[-2]. \tag{7}
\]

Indeed,

\[
i^!L_0\simeq
L_0|_\Gamma\otimes\omega_\Gamma[-2],
\]

and the definition \(W=\omega_\Gamma\otimes L_0|_\Gamma\) cancels every
line factor. Since \(\Gamma\) is connected,

\[
\operatorname{Ext}^2_X(i_*W,L_0)
\simeq H^0(\Gamma,\mathcal O_\Gamma)\simeq\mathbb C. \tag{8}
\]

The principal elliptic fibration has
\(R^0\pi_*\mathcal O_X=R^1\pi_*\mathcal O_X=\mathcal O_S\). On the
degree-two K3 base,

\[
H^0(S,\mathcal O_S(-H))=H^1(S,\mathcal O_S(-H))=0.
\]

The total-degree-one part of Leray therefore gives

\[
H^1(X,L_0)=0. \tag{9}
\]

Apply \(\operatorname{RHom}(-,L_0)\) to (5). Equations (8) and (9) yield an
injection

\[
\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(E,L_0)
\hookrightarrow\operatorname{Ext}^2_X(i_*W,L_0)\simeq\mathbb C. \tag{10}
\]

The class of (1) is nonzero. If it split, then \(E\) would be a rank-two
subsheaf of \(\kappa_{\rm hol}=L_0\oplus E\) with slope zero, whereas

\[
\mu(\kappa_{\rm hol})=-\frac23.
\]

This contradicts the all-balanced-ray stability proved in UST.G3F.
Consequently (10) contains a nonzero element and must be an isomorphism.
This proves (2). The projectivized space of nonzero forward classes is one
point, so the forward extension carries no continuous extension-class knob.

## 4. Reverse Ext Nonvanishing

Twisting (5) by \(\mathcal O_X(H)\) gives

\[
0\longrightarrow E(H)\longrightarrow\mathcal O_X(H)^{\oplus2}
\longrightarrow i_*\omega_\Gamma\longrightarrow0. \tag{11}
\]

The q79 polarization has \(H^2=2\), hence

\[
h^0(X,\mathcal O_X(H))=h^0(S,\mathcal O_S(H))=3. \tag{12}
\]

For the connected Gorenstein genus-seven curve,

\[
h^0(\Gamma,\omega_\Gamma)=7. \tag{13}
\]

The map on global sections in (11) therefore has a six-dimensional source
and a seven-dimensional target. Its cokernel has dimension at least one, and
the long exact sequence injects that cokernel into \(H^1(X,E(H))\). Since
\(L_0\) is locally free,

\[
\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(L_0,E)
\simeq H^1(X,E(H))\ne0. \tag{14}
\]

This proves (3) without assuming maximal rank for the six-to-seven map.
Unlike the forward case, the current data do not determine the complete
dimension or projectivized moduli of the reverse Ext space.

## 5. What This Changes

For every reverse class \(\xi\), additivity in K-theory gives

\[
[\kappa_{\rm rev,\xi}]=[E]+[L_0]=[\kappa_{\rm hol}], \tag{15}
\]

so the selected topological and Chern rows are preserved. This makes the
reverse-extension route a real same-K-class candidate rather than a formal
empty branch.

The next test is finite and falsifiable. Decompose
\(\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(L_0,E)\) under the literal clock, shift, shared-line
and analytic-alpha actions. An admissible class must simultaneously:

1. be nonzero and selected without observed inputs;
2. produce a locally free or accepted perfect middle object;
3. restrict on the exceptional fibers to the UST.G3G stable repair;
4. retain the locked analytic-alpha Cech law;
5. admit the two UST.G3H generator maps and selected commutator;
6. transform to the determinant-one \(P(3,9)\), \(k=0\) hidden rows.

If no such reverse class exists, the reverse route is excluded and the
direct point-modification/global-repaired-complex route remains. If one does
exist, its transform is the leading hidden \(W_9\) candidate. Neither outcome
is asserted here.

